    "description": "Iranian basketry includes containers, mats, trays, and screens made through several ways of combining plant fibres. Kapu from Khuzestan provides a particularly well-documented example of material knowledge, household learning, and adaptation to new markets.",
    "tags": ["Basketry", "Kapu", "Khuzestan", "Plant fibres", "Women's craftsmanship"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:34:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:34:43Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Basket Weaving in Iran: Regional Fibres and Khuzestan Kapu",
      "description": "Discover Iranian basketry through regional plant materials, kapu construction, household apprenticeship, and the experiences of a Khuzestan weaver."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "regional-materials",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A family of practical objects",
        "content": "Basketry in Iran extends beyond baskets. Encyclopaedia Iranica's account of twentieth-century crafts includes mats, tent screens, trays, fans, and lidded containers made by interlacing, twining, or coiling fibres. It associates reed baskets with southern lowlands around Behbahan, Bushehr, and Bandar Abbas, and records relief-patterned rush mats in Mazandaran on the Caspian coast. Qashqai storage boxes combine reeds with coloured wool. This historical survey shows why the category is best approached through materials, construction, and use rather than one national basket shape. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "kapu-construction",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Kapu: building around a plant-fibre core",
        "content": "Kapu is associated particularly with Dezful and nearby villages in Khuzestan, southwestern Iran. Visit Iran describes a flexible plant material called kertak forming a core around which palm leaflets are worked. Young, pale leaflets are soaked before use. Coloured wool can cover parts of the surface or create a more extensively covered exterior. Material and colour are therefore connected to construction, rather than being decoration applied to a finished rigid vessel. [s2]\n\nThe account distinguishes versions using palm leaflets, versions covered with yarn, and combinations of the two. It also describes an expanded range of sizes and objects, including fruit bowls, coasters, and vases, responding to market demand. These variations show how a recognizable craft can accommodate new uses. The same source warns that the core is sensitive to humidity, an important qualification to general claims about the durability of plant materials. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "learning-and-agency",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning through company and correction",
        "content": "An interview published by the retailer Zan Artefacts offers a maker's perspective. The weaver, identified by the protective pseudonym Armaya, recalls watching her mother, aunt, and other relatives before practising alongside friends. Her account of an older woman correcting a difficult basket lid makes apprenticeship concrete: seeing a finished object was only part of learning how to resolve a construction problem. [s3]\n\nShe describes weaving as both pleasurable work and a source of money she can spend independently. Her stated household uses include storing sewing equipment, jewellery, and other small possessions; she also makes coordinated baskets for brides. These details connect basketry to domestic organization and personal decisions. They are one person's testimony, published by a business selling the work, and cannot establish typical earnings or working conditions across Khuzestan. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "new-settings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From household container to international design object",
        "content": "The Victoria and Albert Museum's shop provides a specific example of kapu reaching an overseas retail setting. Its listing credits Zan Artefacts and identifies date-palm leaves, kertak, and wool as the materials of a small Iranian basket. The record supports the existence of that product and sales channel; it does not measure the size of the export trade or the maker's share of the retail price. [s4]\n\nRead alongside Armaya's account, this example suggests a useful way to understand continuity: follow the relationship between an object's construction, the maker's choices, and its intended use. Basketry can remain rooted in learned skills while entering a different setting and acquiring a new audience. That interpretation does not require assuming that every basket follows an unchanged ancient pattern. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
