    "page_id": "topic-2-8-6",
    "page_path": "/explore/culture/clothing-and-everyday-crafts/traditional-pottery/",
    "name": "Traditional Pottery",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/culture/clothing-and-everyday-crafts/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Traditional pottery in Iran encompasses varied methods of shaping, decorating, and firing clay. Kalpurgan's unglazed vessels offer a focused example, while archaeological evidence provides a much longer history that must be distinguished from claims of uninterrupted modern continuity.",
    "tags": ["Pottery", "Ceramics", "Kalpurgan", "Baluch craftsmanship", "Archaeology"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:34:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:34:43Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Traditional Pottery in Iran: Clay, Kalpurgan and Historical Evidence",
      "description": "Understand Iranian pottery through archaeological context, regional forming and firing methods, and the unglazed vessels made by women in Kalpurgan."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "archaeological-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Everyday vessels as historical evidence",
        "content": "Pottery helps archaeologists investigate ordinary life because vessels were widely used and ceramic fragments survive well. The Milwaukee Public Museum explains that their analysis can illuminate activities, economies, beliefs, and social organization. Its overview places some early Iranian pottery in the Neolithic period, around the seventh millennium BCE. This is a broad archaeological timescale, not a founding date for a present-day craft community. [s1]\n\nBefore the potter's wheel, vessels could be built by coiling clay or forming it around a mould. The museum describes similarities between early pottery from the Iranian plateau and neighbouring regions to the west, including Mesopotamia and Anatolia. It also discusses the appearance of painted geometric, animal, and symbolic decoration. These comparisons place pottery within regional exchange and experimentation rather than treating it as an isolated national invention. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-methods",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Several ways to shape and fire clay",
        "content": "Encyclopaedia Iranica's historical survey records different forming and firing arrangements within Iran. Near Kalpurgan it describes women shaping vessels on a simple rotating support made from nested saucers with sand between them. Its account of Asalem in the Talesh region of northern Iran describes similar supports but different clay preparation and firing enclosures. These observations document local technical choices; they should not be turned into a ranking of communities as more or less advanced. [s2]\n\nThe survey also reports competition from plastics, metal vessels, and inexpensive imported ceramics. Because its evidence concerns twentieth-century crafts, this is historical context rather than a measurement of market conditions in 2026. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "kalpurgan",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Kalpurgan: an unglazed regional tradition",
        "content": "Kalpurgan is a village in Sistan and Baluchestan, southeastern Iran, near the Pakistan border. Visit Iran describes pottery made mainly by women without a potter's wheel. The vessels are unglazed, with clay that becomes red after firing and geometric or abstract painted decoration. Bowls, jars, jugs, and drinking vessels are among the forms it identifies. [s3]\n\nThe account describes drying the shaped vessels before applying a mineral pigment prepared from a stone called tituk. The stone is ground, mixed with water, and applied using a thin palm twig. Firing darkens the painted designs. Men are described as supplying the clay, while women undertake the principal forming and decorative work. This division of tasks shows that the finished vessel depends on linked activities, including obtaining and preparing materials. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "kalpurgan-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The documented Kalpurgan production sequence",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Obtain local clay\"] -->|\"Prepare material for shaping\"| B[\"Form vessels by hand\"]\n  B -->|\"Dry before decoration\"| C[\"Dry shaped vessels\"]\n  C -->|\"Apply mineral pigment with a palm twig\"| D[\"Paint geometric designs\"]\n  D -->|\"Harden vessels and darken decoration\"| E[\"Fire vessels\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows show the sequence described by Visit Iran. This is a regional account, not a universal recipe for Iranian pottery.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recognizing continuity without overstating it",
        "content": "Visit Iran compares Kalpurgan pottery with archaeological finds and presents its methods as an ancient inheritance. Resemblance, however, does not by itself demonstrate an unbroken sequence of identical practice. The defensible distinction is between the long archaeological history of pottery in Iran and the specifically documented techniques of a living community. Keeping both in view allows the vessels to be understood as skilled work with historical depth, without assigning their makers an unsupported claim of technological immutability. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
